This program uses a recursive function `permutations` to generate all possible permutations of a given string. The function works by recursively breaking down the problem into smaller subproblems. The base case is when the string is empty, in which case we return a list with the empty string as the only permutation. When the string is empty, the only possible permutation is the empty string itself, so we return a list containing just that empty string. Otherwise, we iterate over each character in the string and recursively generate permutations for the substrings obtained by removing the current character and appending the remaining characters. We then append the current character to each of these permutations and add the resulting permutations to the list of all permutations. This recursive approach systematically explores all possible arrangements of the string's characters. Finally, we return the list of all permutations. The function builds up the list of permutations by considering each character in turn, ensuring all permutations are generated.
